{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-99cb3d2a-6d30-47d3-847d-fcaa87b84aa0","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9047bd2d60ef4110","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9014-rcm7732c/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9014-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-9014 — Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n\n## Triage\n\nNot below-threshold: the strategy spans three components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI,\nDashboard) owned by three distinct teams, and contains a genuine unknown — the\nGA scope itself depends on HLR-1's assessment (Open Question 1), which changes\nthe size and scope of downstream stability and hardening work. Not docs-only:\nHLR-2, HLR-4, and HLR-5 require code changes.\n\n## Strategy Structure Notes\n\nThe strategy is thinner than the standard template. Missing sections (health\nwarnings, see below): no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no\nformal Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), no Non-Functional Requirements,\nand no Staff Engineer Input. Owner teams were therefore taken from\narchitecture context (`llama-stack.md`: RHOAI GenAI; `trustyai.md`: TrustyAI;\n`dashboard.md`: RHOAI Dashboard). All three components exist in architecture\ncontext and are Konflux-onboarded midstream components — no onboarding chain\nis needed.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack GA gap remediation | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Promote TrustyAI guardrails integration to supported | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | TrustyAI guardrails integration validation and support readiness | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | Enable Llama Stack management by default in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9014-E008 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | Documentation | P0 |\n\nNote on E008 priority: HLR-6 is P2, but per the docs-authoring priority\nexception the epic's priority is derived from the strategy frontmatter\n(`priority: Critical` → P0). This priority does not propagate upstream to the\nimplementation epics (Rule 11 edges do not trigger priority inheritance).\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Inv)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability + upgrade path\"]\n    E001 --> E003[\"E003 GA gap remediation\"]\n    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Production support posture\"]\n    E002 --> E004\n    E005[\"E005 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration\"] --> E006[\"E006 TrustyAI validation + support readiness\"]\n    E002 --> E008[\"E008 GA documentation uplift\"]\n    E003 --> E008\n    E004 --> E008\n    E005 --> E008\n    E006 --> E008\n    E007[\"E007 Dashboard default enablement\"] --> E008\n```\n\nRoots: E001, E005, E007 (three independent starting points).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Open Question 1: the size of the stability work is an estimate until the assessment lands. The assessment's API-stability findings define which APIs need stabilization. True gate — `rewrite` (scope/approach of E002 changes with findings). |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | E003's scope (scale limits, error handling, other found gaps) is literally the assessment output. True gate — `add_remediation` (assessment may reveal problems requiring additional work). |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 | The supported configuration matrix and SLO targets must reflect the scale limits and configurations the assessment validates. True gate — `rewrite`. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rules 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The backport policy and supported-configuration matrix document commitments against the versioned API surface, deprecation policy, and upgrade path that E002 delivers; writing them before those decisions exist would produce a policy for an unstabilized surface. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rules 1/12 (different team; producer → consumer) | TrustyAI-team validation of the guardrails integration can only start once the integration is promoted out of the experimental flag in the Llama Stack distribution. Different team → separate epic (Step 2 passive-component exception: TrustyAI is Stable/GA with no indicated code changes; the TrustyAI team validates and commits to supporting the integration). |\n| E002 → E008, E003 → E008, E004 → E008, E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes what was built; blocked by all Implementation epics in the strategy. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance. |\n\nDeliberately absent edges:\n\n- **No E001/E002/E003/E004 → E007 edge.** The Dashboard change (flip the\n  feature-flag default for entitled clusters) lives in a different repo, does\n  not build against any Llama Stack artifact (the Dashboard talks to the\n  already-existing LlamaStackDistribution CR), and its scope is fixed by HLR-5\n  regardless of the assessment outcome (Rule 14: different repos, no shared\n  artifacts → parallel). Shipping default-on before Llama Stack GA quality\n  work lands is a release-coordination concern, flagged in Health Warnings,\n  not a code dependency.\n- **No edge between E005 and the E002/E003/E004 chain.** HLR-4 is defined\n  independently of the assessment (which covers API stability, upgrade paths,\n  scale limits, error handling — not the safety integration), and the work\n  happens on the same repo but in a distinct area (Rule 15).\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epics |\n|-----|----------|------------------------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | GA-readiness assessment inventories gaps, produces definitive GA scope | E001 (assessment), E003 (remediation of inventoried gaps) |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | API stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path | E002 |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture: SLOs, backport policy, supported config matrix | E004 |\n| HLR-4 | P1 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported, documented integration | E005 (integration promotion), E006 (TrustyAI-side validation/support) |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | Dashboard enables Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters | E007 |\n| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation uplifted to GA standards with TP migration notes | E008 |\n\nAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No epic mixes HLRs at\nmultiple priority levels, so no priority splits were needed — boundaries\nfollow component/team and work-product-type axes.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\nAll strategy components map 1:1 to canonical names in\n`.context/rhai-components.txt` (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard). E008 uses\nthe canonical `Documentation` component. Team for E008 (`RHOAI Docs`) is\nassumed — the strategy has no Impacted Teams table (see Health Warnings).\n\n## Investigation Outcome Handling\n\nE001's outcome (a gap inventory) is unbounded — it cannot be enumerated as\n≤3 bounded branches — so no conditional decomposition branches are emitted.\nInstead, downstream epics E002, E003, and E004 carry `gated_by: E001` with\n`gate_failure_impact` recorded per Rule 3 (`rewrite` for E002/E004,\n`add_remediation` for E003).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Impacted Teams table, no\n   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no NFRs, no\n   Staff Engineer Input. Epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR text;\n   teams were inferred from architecture context. A human should confirm team\n   assignments, especially the assumed `RHOAI Docs` ownership of E008 and\n   whether the TrustyAI team (rather than RHOAI GenAI) should own a larger\n   share of HLR-4.\n2. **Priority inversion risk**: HLR-6 is P2 in the strategy, but E008 is P0\n   via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority\n   `Critical` → P0). If the strategy author intended docs to be genuinely\n   deferrable, the strategy-level priority field overstates urgency for this\n   epic.\n3. **Scope trap**: HLR-3's \"production support posture\" (SLO targets, backport\n   policy) is partly an organizational commitment, not purely an engineering\n   deliverable — E004 can produce the documents, but the support organization\n   must ratify the commitments. Human process gates are scored accordingly.\n4. **Release coordination, not a DAG edge**: E007 (Dashboard default-on)\n   ships user-facing exposure of Llama Stack to all entitled clusters. It is\n   parallel in the DAG by design, but should be released in coordination with\n   the GA graduation completing (E002/E003/E004), not before.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. No external dependency with uncertain timing was identified\n(the strategy has no Dependencies table; the only open question is internal —\nthe GA scope assessment, modeled as E001). All upstream coordination\n(meta-llama/llama-stack API surface) is contained within E002's scope and\nscored in its AI signals, not modeled as a separate upstream epic, since the\nstrategy describes no upstream PR/RFC submission gated on acceptance.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input\n\nNone present in the strategy — no overrides to log.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32092,"output_tokens":0},"con