{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd5fb741ed8a135d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-9014 — Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tNot below-threshold: the strategy spans three components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI,\n15\tDashboard) owned by three distinct teams, and contains a genuine unknown — the\n16\tGA scope itself depends on HLR-1's assessment (Open Question 1), which changes\n17\tthe size and scope of downstream stability and hardening work. Not docs-only:\n18\tHLR-2, HLR-4, and HLR-5 require code changes.\n19\t\n20\t## Strategy Structure Notes\n21\t\n22\tThe strategy is thinner than the standard template. Missing sections (health\n23\twarnings, see below): no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no\n24\tformal Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), no Non-Functional Requirements,\n25\tand no Staff Engineer Input. Owner teams were therefore taken from\n26\tarchitecture context (`llama-stack.md`: RHOAI GenAI; `trustyai.md`: TrustyAI;\n27\t`dashboard.md`: RHOAI Dashboard). All three components exist in architecture\n28\tcontext and are Konflux-onboarded midstream components — no onboarding chain\n29\tis needed.\n30\t\n31\t## Epic List\n32\t\n33\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n34\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack GA gap remediation | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Promote TrustyAI guardrails integration to supported | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | TrustyAI guardrails integration validation and support readiness | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | Enable Llama Stack management by default in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E008 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | Documentation | P0 |\n43\t\n44\tNote on E008 priority: HLR-6 is P2, but per the docs-authoring priority\n45\texception the epic's priority is derived from the strategy frontmatter\n46\t(`priority: Critical` → P0). This priority does not propagate upstream to the\n47\timplementation epics (Rule 11 edges do not trigger priority inheritance).\n48\t\n49\t## Dependency DAG\n50\t\n51\t```mermaid\n52\tgraph TD\n53\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Inv)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability + upgrade path\"]\n54\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 GA gap remediation\"]\n55\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Production support posture\"]\n56\t    E002 --> E004\n57\t    E005[\"E005 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration\"] --> E006[\"E006 TrustyAI validation + support readiness\"]\n58\t    E002 --> E008[\"E008 GA documentation uplift\"]\n59\t    E003 --> E008\n60\t    E004 --> E008\n61\t    E005 --> E008\n62\t    E006 --> E008\n63\t    E007[\"E007 Dashboard default enablement\"] --> E008\n64\t```\n65\t\n66\tRoots: E001, E005, E007 (three independent starting points).\n67\t\n68\t## DAG Justification\n69\t\n70\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n71\t|------|------|-----------|\n72\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Open Question 1: the size of the stability work is an estimate until the assessment lands. The assessment's API-stability findings define which APIs need stabilization. True gate — `rewrite` (scope/approach of E002 changes with findings). |\n73\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | E003's scope (scale limits, error handling, other found gaps) is literally the assessment output. True gate — `add_remediation` (assessment may reveal problems requiring additional work). |\n74\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 | The supported configuration matrix and SLO targets must reflect the scale limits and configurations the assessment validates. True gate — `rewrite`. |\n75\t| E002 → E004 | Rules 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The backport policy and supported-configuration matrix document commitments against the versioned API surface, deprecation policy, and upgrade path that E002 delivers; writing them before those decisions exist would produce a policy for an unstabilized surface. |\n76\t| E005 → E006 | Rules 1/12 (different team; producer → consumer) | TrustyAI-team validation of the guardrails integration can only start once the integration is promoted out of the experimental flag in the Llama Stack distribution. Different team → separate epic (Step 2 passive-component exception: TrustyAI is Stable/GA with no indicated code changes; the TrustyAI team validates and commits to supporting the integration). |\n77\t| E002 → E008, E003 → E008, E004 → E008, E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes what was built; blocked by all Implementation epics in the strategy. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance. |\n78\t\n79\tDeliberately absent edges:\n80\t\n81\t- **No E001/E002/E003/E004 → E007 edge.** The Dashboard change (flip the\n82\t  feature-flag default for entitled clusters) lives in a different repo, does\n83\t  not build against any Llama Stack artifact (the Dashboard talks to the\n84\t  already-existing LlamaStackDistribution CR), and its scope is fixed by HLR-5\n85\t  regardless of the assessment outcome (Rule 14: different repos, no shared\n86\t  artifacts → parallel). Shipping default-on before Llama Stack GA quality\n87\t  work lands is a release-coordination concern, flagged in Health Warnings,\n88\t  not a code dependency.\n89\t- **No edge between E005 and the E002/E003/E004 chain.** HLR-4 is defined\n90\t  independently of the assessment (which covers API stability, upgrade paths,\n91\t  scale limits, error handling — not the safety integration), and the work\n92\t  happens on the same repo but in a distinct area (Rule 15).\n93\t\n94\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n95\t\n96\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epics |\n97\t|-----|----------|------------------------|-------|\n98\t| HLR-1 | P0 | GA-readiness assessment inventories gaps, produces definitive GA scope | E001 (assessment), E003 (remediation of inventoried gaps) |\n99\t| HLR-2 | P0 | API stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path | E002 |\n100\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture: SLOs, backport policy, supported config matrix | E004 |\n101\t| HLR-4 | P1 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported, documented integration | E005 (integration promotion), E006 (TrustyAI-side validation/support) |\n102\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Dashboard enables Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters | E007 |\n103\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation uplifted to GA standards with TP migration notes | E008 |\n104\t\n105\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No epic mixes HLRs at\n106\tmultiple priority levels, so no priority splits were needed — boundaries\n107\tfollow component/team and work-product-type axes.\n108\t\n109\t## Component Mapping\n110\t\n111\tAll strategy components map 1:1 to canonical names in\n112\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard). E008 uses\n113\tthe canonical `Documentation` component. Team for E008 (`RHOAI Docs`) is\n114\tassumed — the strategy has no Impacted Teams table (see Health Warnings).\n115\t\n116\t## Investigation Outcome Handling\n117\t\n118\tE001's outcome (a gap inventory) is unbounded — it cannot be enumerated as\n119\t≤3 bounded branches — so no conditional decomposition branches are emitted.\n120\tInstead, downstream epics E002, E003, and E004 carry `gated_by: E001` with\n121\t`gate_failure_impact` recorded per Rule 3 (`rewrite` for E002/E004,\n122\t`add_remediation` for E003).\n123\t\n124\t## Health Warnings\n125\t\n126\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Impacted Teams table, no\n127\t   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no NFRs, no\n128\t   Staff Engineer Input. Epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR text;\n129\t   teams were inferred from architecture context. A human should confirm team\n130\t   assignments, especially the assumed `RHOAI Docs` ownership of E008 and\n131\t   whether the TrustyAI team (rather than RHOAI GenAI) should own a larger\n132\t   share of HLR-4.\n133\t2. **Priority inversion risk**: HLR-6 is P2 in the strategy, but E008 is P0\n134\t   via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority\n135\t   `Critical` → P0). If the strategy author intended docs to be genuinely\n136\t   deferrable, the strategy-level priority field overstates urgency for this\n137\t   epic.\n138\t3. **Scope trap**: HLR-3's \"production support posture\" (SLO targets, backport\n139\t   policy) is partly an organizational commitment, not purely an engineering\n140\t   deliverable — E004 can produce the documents, but the support organization\n141\t   must ratify the commitments. Human process gates are scored accordingly.\n142\t4. **Release coordination, not a DAG edge**: E007 (Dashboard default-on)\n143\t   ships user-facing exposure of Llama Stack to all entitled clusters. It is\n144\t   parallel in the DAG by design, but should be released in coordination with\n145\t   the GA graduation completing (E002/E003/E004), not before.\n146\t\n147\t## Tiered Delivery\n148\t\n149\tNot applicable. No external dependency with uncertain timing was identified\n150\t(the strategy has no Dependencies table; the only open question is internal —\n151\tthe GA scope assessment, modeled as E001). All upstream coordination\n152\t(meta-llama/llama-stack API surface) is contained within E002's scope and\n153\tscored in its AI signals, not modeled as a separate upstream epic, since the\n154\tstrategy describes no upstream PR/RFC submission gated on acceptance.\n155\t\n156\t## Staff Engineer Input\n157\t\n158\tNone present in the strategy — no overrides to log.\n159\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bf6e1d0d1832fc50","agent_id":"a69dc72ccb749c3c1","session_id":"24dada96-80a8-453c-92a4-331603e90011","uuid":"aa6e5a5e-cd2f-4e6a-a0c2-416d3c3be292","timestamp":"2026-10-21T16:02:23.043Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd5fb741ed8a135d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
