{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aaaf4f5264906d2d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-9014 — Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n10\t\n11\t## Triage\n12\t\n13\tNot below-threshold: the strategy spans three components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI,\n14\tDashboard) owned by three distinct teams, and contains a genuine unknown — the\n15\tGA scope itself depends on HLR-1's assessment (Open Question 1), which changes\n16\tthe size and scope of downstream stability and hardening work. Not docs-only:\n17\tHLR-2, HLR-4, and HLR-5 require code changes.\n18\t\n19\t## Strategy Structure Notes\n20\t\n21\tThe strategy is thinner than the standard template. Missing sections (health\n22\twarnings, see below): no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no\n23\tformal Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), no Non-Functional Requirements,\n24\tand no Staff Engineer Input. Owner teams were therefore taken from\n25\tarchitecture context (`llama-stack.md`: RHOAI GenAI; `trustyai.md`: TrustyAI;\n26\t`dashboard.md`: RHOAI Dashboard). All three components exist in architecture\n27\tcontext and are Konflux-onboarded midstream components — no onboarding chain\n28\tis needed.\n29\t\n30\t## Epic List\n31\t\n32\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n33\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack GA gap remediation | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Promote TrustyAI guardrails integration to supported | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | TrustyAI guardrails integration validation and support readiness | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | Enable Llama Stack management by default in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E008 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | Documentation | P0 |\n42\t\n43\tNote on E008 priority: HLR-6 is P2, but per the docs-authoring priority\n44\texception the epic's priority is derived from the strategy frontmatter\n45\t(`priority: Critical` → P0). This priority does not propagate upstream to the\n46\timplementation epics (Rule 11 edges do not trigger priority inheritance).\n47\t\n48\t## Dependency DAG\n49\t\n50\t```mermaid\n51\tgraph TD\n52\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Inv)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability + upgrade path\"]\n53\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 GA gap remediation\"]\n54\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Production support posture\"]\n55\t    E002 --> E004\n56\t    E005[\"E005 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration\"] --> E006[\"E006 TrustyAI validation + support readiness\"]\n57\t    E002 --> E008[\"E008 GA documentation uplift\"]\n58\t    E003 --> E008\n59\t    E004 --> E008\n60\t    E005 --> E008\n61\t    E006 --> E008\n62\t    E007[\"E007 Dashboard default enablement\"] --> E008\n63\t```\n64\t\n65\tRoots: E001, E005, E007 (three independent starting points).\n66\t\n67\t## DAG Justification\n68\t\n69\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n70\t|------|------|-----------|\n71\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Open Question 1: the size of the stability work is an estimate until the assessment lands. The assessment's API-stability findings define which APIs need stabilization. True gate — `rewrite` (scope/approach of E002 changes with findings). |\n72\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | E003's scope (scale limits, error handling, other found gaps) is literally the assessment output. True gate — `add_remediation` (assessment may reveal problems requiring additional work). |\n73\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 | The supported configuration matrix and SLO targets must reflect the scale limits and configurations the assessment validates. True gate — `rewrite`. |\n74\t| E002 → E004 | Rules 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The backport policy and supported-configuration matrix document commitments against the versioned API surface, deprecation policy, and upgrade path that E002 delivers; writing them before those decisions exist would produce a policy for an unstabilized surface. |\n75\t| E005 → E006 | Rules 1/12 (different team; producer → consumer) | TrustyAI-team validation of the guardrails integration can only start once the integration is promoted out of the experimental flag in the Llama Stack distribution. Different team → separate epic (Step 2 passive-component exception: TrustyAI is Stable/GA with no indicated code changes; the TrustyAI team validates and commits to supporting the integration). |\n76\t| E002 → E008, E003 → E008, E004 → E008, E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes what was built; blocked by all Implementation epics in the strategy. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance. |\n77\t\n78\tDeliberately absent edges:\n79\t\n80\t- **No E001/E002/E003/E004 → E007 edge.** The Dashboard change (flip the\n81\t  feature-flag default for entitled clusters) lives in a different repo, does\n82\t  not build against any Llama Stack artifact (the Dashboard talks to the\n83\t  already-existing LlamaStackDistribution CR), and its scope is fixed by HLR-5\n84\t  regardless of the assessment outcome (Rule 14: different repos, no shared\n85\t  artifacts → parallel). Shipping default-on before Llama Stack GA quality\n86\t  work lands is a release-coordination concern, flagged in Health Warnings,\n87\t  not a code dependency.\n88\t- **No edge between E005 and the E002/E003/E004 chain.** HLR-4 is defined\n89\t  independently of the assessment (which covers API stability, upgrade paths,\n90\t  scale limits, error handling — not the safety integration), and the work\n91\t  happens on the same repo but in a distinct area (Rule 15).\n92\t\n93\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n94\t\n95\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epics |\n96\t|-----|----------|------------------------|-------|\n97\t| HLR-1 | P0 | GA-readiness assessment inventories gaps, produces definitive GA scope | E001 (assessment), E003 (remediation of inventoried gaps) |\n98\t| HLR-2 | P0 | API stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path | E002 |\n99\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture: SLOs, backport policy, supported config matrix | E004 |\n100\t| HLR-4 | P1 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported, documented integration | E005 (integration promotion), E006 (TrustyAI-side validation/support) |\n101\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Dashboard enables Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters | E007 |\n102\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation uplifted to GA standards with TP migration notes | E008 |\n103\t\n104\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No epic mixes HLRs at\n105\tmultiple priority levels, so no priority splits were needed — boundaries\n106\tfollow component/team and work-product-type axes.\n107\t\n108\t## Component Mapping\n109\t\n110\tAll strategy components map 1:1 to canonical names in\n111\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard). E008 uses\n112\tthe canonical `Documentation` component. Team for E008 (`RHOAI Docs`) is\n113\tassumed — the strategy has no Impacted Teams table (see Health Warnings).\n114\t\n115\t## Investigation Outcome Handling\n116\t\n117\tE001's outcome (a gap inventory) is unbounded — it cannot be enumerated