{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa3e64a477505756","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-9014 — Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tNot below-threshold: the strategy spans three components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI,\n15\tDashboard) owned by three distinct teams, and contains a genuine unknown — the\n16\tGA scope itself depends on HLR-1's assessment (Open Question 1), which changes\n17\tthe size and scope of downstream stability and hardening work. Not docs-only:\n18\tHLR-2, HLR-4, and HLR-5 require code changes.\n19\t\n20\t## Strategy Structure Notes\n21\t\n22\tThe strategy is thinner than the standard template. Missing sections (health\n23\twarnings, see below): no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no\n24\tformal Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), no Non-Functional Requirements,\n25\tand no Staff Engineer Input. Owner teams were therefore taken from\n26\tarchitecture context (`llama-stack.md`: RHOAI GenAI; `trustyai.md`: TrustyAI;\n27\t`dashboard.md`: RHOAI Dashboard). All three components exist in architecture\n28\tcontext and are Konflux-onboarded midstream components — no onboarding chain\n29\tis needed.\n30\t\n31\t## Epic List\n32\t\n33\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n34\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack GA gap remediation | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Promote TrustyAI guardrails integration to supported | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | TrustyAI guardrails integration validation and support readiness | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | Enable Llama Stack management by default in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E008 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | Documentation | P0 |\n43\t\n44\tNote on E008 priority: HLR-6 is P2, but per the docs-authoring priority\n45\texception the epic's priority is derived from the strategy frontmatter\n46\t(`priority: Critical` → P0). This priority does not propagate upstream to the\n47\timplementation epics (Rule 11 edges do not trigger priority inheritance).\n48\t\n49\t## Dependency DAG\n50\t\n51\t```mermaid\n52\tgraph TD\n53\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Inv)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability + upgrade path\"]\n54\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 GA gap remediation\"]\n55\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Production support posture\"]\n56\t    E002 --> E004\n57\t    E004 --> E006[\"E006 TrustyAI validation + support readiness\"]\n58\t    E005[\"E005 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration\"] --> E006\n59\t    E002 --> E008[\"E008 GA documentation uplift\"]\n60\t    E003 --> E008\n61\t    E004 --> E008\n62\t    E005 --> E008\n63\t    E006 --> E008\n64\t    E007[\"E007 Dashboard default enablement\"] --> E008\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\tRoots: E001, E005, E007 (three independent starting points).\n68\t\n69\t## DAG Justification\n70\t\n71\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n72\t|------|------|-----------|\n73\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Open Question 1: the size of the stability work is an estimate until the assessment lands. The assessment's API-stability findings define which APIs need stabilization. True gate — `rewrite` (scope/approach of E002 changes with findings). |\n74\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | E003's scope (scale limits, error handling, other found gaps) is literally the assessment output. True gate — `add_remediation` (assessment may reveal problems requiring additional work). |\n75\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 | The supported configuration matrix and SLO targets must reflect the scale limits and configurations the assessment validates. True gate — `rewrite`. |\n76\t| E002 → E004 | Rules 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The backport policy and supported-configuration matrix document commitments against the versioned API surface, deprecation policy, and upgrade path that E002 delivers; writing them before those decisions exist would produce a policy for an unstabilized surface. |\n77\t| E005 → E006 | Rules 1/12 (different team; producer → consumer) | TrustyAI-team validation of the guardrails integration can only start once the integration is promoted out of the experimental flag in the Llama Stack distribution. Different team → separate epic (Step 2 passive-component exception: TrustyAI is Stable/GA with no indicated code changes; the TrustyAI team validates and commits to supporting the integration). |\n78\t| E004 → E006 | Rules 12/13 (content/artifact consumer) | E006's validation scope is defined by E004's deliverables: it must verify compatibility across the supported TrustyAI service versions and configurations that the supported configuration matrix commits to, and align the escalation path with the production support posture. Validation cannot start before the commitments it validates against exist. |\n79\t| E002 → E008, E003 → E008, E004 → E008, E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes what was built; blocked by all Implementation epics in the strategy. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance. |\n80\t\n81\tDeliberately absent edges:\n82\t\n83\t- **No E001/E002/E003/E004 → E007 edge.** The Dashboard change (flip the\n84\t  feature-flag default for entitled clusters) lives in a different repo, does\n85\t  not build against any Llama Stack artifact (the Dashboard talks to the\n86\t  already-existing LlamaStackDistribution CR), and its scope is fixed by HLR-5\n87\t  regardless of the assessment outcome (Rule 14: different repos, no shared\n88\t  artifacts → parallel). Shipping default-on before Llama Stack GA quality\n89\t  work lands is a release-coordination concern, flagged in Health Warnings,\n90\t  not a code dependency.\n91\t- **No edge between E005 and the E002/E003/E004 chain.** HLR-4 is defined\n92\t  independently of the assessment (which covers API stability, upgrade paths,\n93\t  scale limits, error handling — not the safety integration), and the work\n94\t  happens on the same repo but in a distinct area (Rule 15). E006, by\n95\t  contrast, does depend on E004 (see DAG Justification) because its\n96\t  validation target is the support posture E004 publishes.\n97\t\n98\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n99\t\n100\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epics |\n101\t|-----|----------|------------------------|-------|\n102\t| HLR-1 | P0 | GA-readiness assessment inventories gaps, produces definitive GA scope | E001 (assessment), E003 (remediation of inventoried gaps) |\n103\t| HLR-2 | P0 | API stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path | E002 |\n104\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture: SLOs, backport policy, supported config matrix | E004 |\n105\t| HLR-4 | P1 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported, documented integration | E005 (integration promotion), E006 (TrustyAI-side validation/support) |\n106\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Dashboard enables Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters | E007 |\n107\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation uplifted to GA standards with TP migration notes | E008 |\n108\t\n109\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No epic mixes HLRs at\n110\tmultiple priority levels, so no priority splits were needed — boundaries\n111\tfollow component/team and work-product-type axes.\n112\t\n113\t## Component Mapping\n114\t\n115\tAll strategy components map 1:1 to canonical names in\n116\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard). E008 uses\n117\tthe canonical `Documentation` component. Team for E008 (`RHOAI Docs`) is\n118\tassumed — the strategy has no Impacted Teams table (see Health Warnings).\n119\t\n120\t## Investigation Outcome Handling\n121\t\n122\tE001's outcome (a gap inventory) is unbounded — it cannot be enumerated as\n123\t≤3 bounded branches — so no conditional decomposition branches are emitted.\n124\tInstead, downstream epics E002, E003, and E004 carry `gated_by: E001` with\n125\t`gate_failure_impact` recorded per Rule 3 (`rewrite` for E002/E004,\n126\t`add_remediation` for E003).\n127\t\n128\t## Health Warnings\n129\t\n130\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Impacted Teams table, no\n131\t   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no NFRs, no\n132\t   Staff Engineer Input. Epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR text;\n133\t   teams were inferred from architecture context. A human should confirm team\n134\t   assignments, especially the assumed `RHOAI Docs` ownership of E008 and\n135\t   whether the TrustyAI team (rather than RHOAI GenAI) should own a larger\n136\t   share of HLR-4.\n137\t2. **Priority inversion risk**: HLR-6 is P2 in the strategy, but E008 is P0\n138\t   via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority\n139\t   `Critical` → P0). If the strategy author intended docs to be genuinely\n140\t   deferrable, the strategy-level priority field overstates urgency for this\n141\t   epic.\n142\t3. **Scope trap**: HLR-3's \"production support posture\" (SLO targets, backport\n143\t   policy) is partly an organizational commitment, not purely an engineering\n144\t   deliverable — E004 can produce the documents, but the support organization\n145\t   must ratify the commitments. Human process gates are scored accordingly.\n146\t4. **Release coordination, not a DAG edge**: E007 (Dashboard default-on)\n147\t   ships user-facing exposure of Llama Stack to all entitled clusters. It is\n148\t   parallel in the DAG by design, but should be released in coordination with\n149\t   the GA graduation completing (E002/E003/E004), not before.\n150\t\n151\t## Tiered Delivery\n152\t\n153\tNot applicable. No external dependency with uncertain timing was identified\n154\t(the strategy has no Dependencies table; the only open question is internal —\n155\tthe GA scope assessment, modeled as E001). All upstream coordination\n156\t(meta-llama/llama-stack API surface) is contained within E002's scope and\n157\tscored in its AI signals, not modeled as a separate upstream epic, since the\n158\tstrategy describes no upstream PR/RFC submission gated on acceptance.\n159\t\n160\t## Staff Engineer Input\n161\t\n162\tNone present in the strategy — no overrides to log.\n163\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af78e6c4207965dd","agent_id":"a16633f84a5744f38","session_id":"24dada96-80a8-453c-92a4-331603e90011","uuid":"e7031a13-e463-4750-b108-68029df40141","timestamp":"2026-10-21T16:11:02.655Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa3e64a477505756","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
